[?2004l[?2004h> === CLEANUP SCRIPT FIX - May 5 ===
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> PROBLEM:
[?2004l[?2004h> --------
[?2004l[?2004h> The cleanup.sh script was either deleting nothing or deleting everything,
[?2004l[?2004h> including .dSYM bundles we needed to keep.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ROOT CAUSE:
[?2004l[?2004h> -----------
[?2004l[?2004h> Filenames with spaces (e.g., 'MyApp 2.1 (build 447).dSYM') were being
[?2004l[?2004h> piped to 'xargs rm -rf' without proper quoting. When xargs received
[?2004l[?2004h> 'MyApp 2.1 (build 447).dSYM', it split it on whitespace into separate
[?2004l[?2004h> arguments: 'MyApp', '2.1', '(build', '447).dSYM'
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> This caused:
[?2004l[?2004h> - Deletion failures (broken paths don't exist)
[?2004l[?2004h> - Wrong deletions (if partial paths matched other files)
[?2004l[?2004h> - Unpredictable behavior
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> SOLUTION:
[?2004l[?2004h> ---------
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Moved 'rm -rf' inside the while loop where variables are properly quoted
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Added 'read -r' flag to prevent backslash interpretation
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Added debug output to show what's being deleted vs kept
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> KEY CHANGES:
[?2004l[?2004h> ------------
[?2004l[?2004h> OLD: done | xargs rm -rf
[?2004l[?2004h> NEW: rm -rf "$dsym" (inside the loop with proper quoting)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> TESTED:
[?2004l[?2004h> -------
[?2004l[?2004h> ✓ Correctly deletes .dSYM bundles >7 days old without matching .ipa
[?2004l[?2004h> ✓ Preserves .dSYM bundles that have matching .ipa files
[?2004l[?2004h> ✓ Handles filenames with spaces, parentheses, brackets correctly
[?2004l[?2004h> 
