[?2004l=== CLEANUP SCRIPT FIX - May 5 ===

PROBLEM:
--------
The cleanup.sh script was either deleting nothing or deleting everything,
including .dSYM bundles we needed to keep.

ROOT CAUSE:
-----------
Filenames with spaces (e.g., 'MyApp 2.1 (build 447).dSYM') were being
piped to 'xargs rm -rf' without proper quoting. When xargs received
'MyApp 2.1 (build 447).dSYM', it split it on whitespace into separate
arguments: 'MyApp', '2.1', '(build', '447).dSYM'

This caused:
- Deletion failures (broken paths don't exist)
- Wrong deletions (if partial paths matched other files)
- Unpredictable behavior

SOLUTION:
---------
1. Moved 'rm -rf' inside the while loop where variables are properly quoted
2. Added 'read -r' flag to prevent backslash interpretation
3. Added debug output to show what's being deleted vs kept

KEY CHANGES:
------------
OLD: done | xargs rm -rf
NEW: rm -rf "$dsym" (inside the loop with proper quoting)

TESTED:
-------
✓ Correctly deletes .dSYM bundles >7 days old without matching .ipa
✓ Preserves .dSYM bundles that have matching .ipa files
✓ Handles filenames with spaces, parentheses, brackets correctly

Backup of original script saved at: /home/user/scripts/cleanup.sh.bak
